## Build brief

**Concept — ink on paper.** Quill turns a directory of manuscript into a directory of pages, so the page is set as a printed technical document: warm uncoated stock, iron-gall blue-black for links and matched files, vermilion for correction marks (section numerals, excluded files, `strict`), moss for successful output. Structure comes from 1px rules, not from a grid of cards. JetBrains Mono carries everything the machine says; IBM Plex Sans carries the prose.

**Sections, in order:** sticky bar with scroll-spy nav → hero → 01 Install → 02 Quickstart → 03 Configuration → 04 Commands → 05 When it fails → footer.

**Signature component — the config studio (03).** All six keys are rendered as an editable `quill.config.js`: real text fields, editable/removable glob chips, a `strict` switch, a `concurrency` stepper. Beside it, a real resolver (a small glob→regex matcher) recomputes the build live against a sample project: which files matched, which pattern excluded each one, what lands in `dist/`, and a status line that flips from *built 5 files — 1 warning* to *build failed — 1 warning treated as an error* when `strict` goes true. Type, default and description sit in aligned columns next to each key. It's the product doing its job, not a chart about the product.

**Continuity.** That same sample project is the hero figure (`./` → `dist/`, 7 files in, 5 out), then the studio's live subject, and the source of the warning the errors band explains. It changes role and state rather than repeating.

**The one break in the paper field** is section 05 on near-black — that's what the terminal actually looks like. Three error rows, each code + literal message on the first read, `fix` expanded below; all open by default and collapsible.

**Motion.** One hover grammar across the route: the surface darkens one step, border darkens, 120/180ms. Focus rings on everything, one regional entrance (14px, 340ms, triggered against the top of the viewport, never on the first screen), tab-underline transition, file rows settling in when a glob changes, `prefers-reduced-motion` honoured.

**Mobile (390px) is authored, not stacked:** the in/out figure turns vertical with the arrow rotated, install tabs go 2×2 with a full-width 44px copy button, config rows drop to a flowed layout with metadata beneath, the nav becomes a masked scroll rail, the GitHub button and version chip are dropped from the bar.

**Requirements preserved:** nav to all six sections; four install methods with exact commands, switchable, working copy button; quickstart block verbatim; six config keys with type/default/description; four commands with descriptions and every flag; three error codes with messages and fixes; four footer links.

## Verified
Rendered in Chromium at 1440×900 and 390×844 — full-page and per-section captures, tab switching, copy, config editing (added `notes/**`, toggled `strict`), error expansion. Zero horizontal overflow at both widths; no console errors except headless clipboard permission, which is a harness artefact.

Two things worth flagging: outsourced material is limited to the Lucide icon set and two self-hosted open fonts — I deliberately used no photography, since a hero image on a CLI docs page is the fabricated-prop trap and the file listing is the honest picture of what this tool does. And I did not run `dreative finalize`; it isn't installed in this project, so the checks above are what I ran directly.

Implementation complete; human taste verdict: awaiting user review. Worth looking at the desktop full page, the config studio while editing a glob, and 390px.
